1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margarida_Cabral_de_Melo
2 CONT
2 CONT Margarida Cabral de Melo (1570–1631) was a Portuguese noblewoman related to the discoverer of Brazil Pedro Álvares Cabral.[2] In 1599 she settled with her husband and children in Buenos Aires.[3] She was one of the most distinguis
2 CONC hed women in the Río de la Plata, in the early 17th century, owned of luxury homes, farms and vineyards.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her family belonged to illustrious Portuguese lineages, including her royal ancestry through her great-great-grandmother, Dona Beatriz de Meneses, 2nd Countess of Loulé. She and her husband were the ancestors of Argentine politici
2 CONC ans and military, among them Marcelo Torcuato de Alvear, President of the Argentine Republic between 1922 and 1928.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Azores Islands
2 CONT Margarida was born in Santa Maria Island, the daughter of Matias Nunes Cabral and Maria Simões de Melo, belonging to a noble Portuguese family.[6] She was married to Amador Vaz de Alpoim, son of Estevan de Alpoim and Isabel Velha.
2 CONC  She and her family arrived in Buenos Aires from Rio de Janeiro, where they had lived for five years.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Margarida Cabral de Melo and her sister Inês Nunes Cabral (wife of Gil Gonçalves de Moura) were the first women to have political and economic influence in the Río de la Plata. Margarida was perhaps the richest woman in Buenos Air
2 CONC es towards the beginning of 1600. She possessed one of the most luxurious houses in the city, with mahogany furniture, carved silverware, tapestry and among its jewelry, a gold parrot.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT The house of the family Cabral de Alpoim was located at the intersection of the streets Victoria and Balcarce, current neighborhood of Monserrat.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Margarida and her family were linked to the beginnings of cattle raising (cimarron cattle) in the current Argentine territory. She was the owner of a ranch located in the town of Luján, which was managed by her son Manuel Cabral d
2 CONC e Alpoim.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband died in 1617 being buried in the city. He had rendered military services to the Spanish Empire, taking part in some military expeditions led by Hernando Arias de Saavedra.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT She had many sons and daughters, including General Amador Báez de Alpoim, who served as mayor of Buenos Aires, lieutenant governor of Santa Fe and Corrientes,[12] and Matías Cabral de Melo y Alpoim (1593-1645) a well-known presbyt
2 CONC er of Buenos Aires.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestors and descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Belmonte Castle, belonging to the Cabral lineage
2 CONT Margarita Cabral de Melo and his sister were relatives of Pedro Álvares Cabral, discoverer of Brazil and Gonçalo Velho Cabral, discoverer of Azores.[14] His most notorious ancestors were Álvaro Martins Homem 3°Captain of Praia,[15
2 CONC ] and João Fernandes de Andrade, possibly a descendant of Richard, 1st Earl of Cornwall.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is proven that his family descended by several genealogical lines of the kings of Portugal, including Afonso III[17] and John I of Portugal. Through these royal ancestors, their lineage is linked to the House of Plantagenet and
2 CONC  Castile.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT She and her sister were the ancestors of various historical figures linked to the politics and culture of the Río de la Plata like Juan Manuel de Rosas, governor of the province of Buenos Aires between 1835 and 1852,[19] and Justo
2 CONC  José de Urquiza, first President of the Argentine Confederation.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT Other famous descendants of this family include José Gervasio Artigas, a Uruguayan patriot who participated in the War of Independence,[21] and Carlos María de Alvear, a military and politician who served as Supreme Director of th
2 CONC e United Provinces of the Río de la Plata in 1815.[22]
2 CONT
2 CONT His sister Inés Nunes Cabral and Gil Gonçalves de Moura were the ancestors of the writer and poet Jorge Luis Borges.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT
